diagnosis_text:
The intersite link between router0 and router1 (10.16.1.0/24) is not down. Both ends are up and passing traffic: router0 eth2 has 10.16.1.1/24 and is UP/LOWER_UP; router1 eth2 has 10.16.1.2/24 and is UP/LOWER_UP. A direct ping from router0 to 10.16.1.2 succeeds with 0% loss, confirming the physical/Layer-3 link is healthy. The observed symptom at Location A (PC4/PC5, Laptop0) stems from routing on router0, not a link failure. PC4’s ping to a remote subnet returns “Destination Net Unreachable” from 172.16.10.1 (router0), and router0’s routing table lacks routes for remote networks (e.g., 192.168.200.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24). On router0, the FRRouting daemon is failed (systemctl shows frr.service Active: failed), and its FRR config is effectively empty. Conversely, router1’s FRR is running and learning/advertising routes via RIP. With FRR down on router0, it no longer participates in RIP nor installs learned routes, so it cannot forward Location A traffic beyond its local VLANs and point-to-point statics. Root cause: FRR service failure on router0 during/after maintenance, leaving router0 without dynamic routes to the rest of the network. Corrective fix: Restore routing on router0 by starting FRR and reapplying the RIP configuration (e.g., router rip; network 10.0.0.0), or add appropriate static/default routes via 10.16.1.2. After FRR is running, verify routes to remote subnets appear on router0 and that Location A regains reachability. No down interface exists on the router0–router1 link. === END ===